WEBVTT

00:00:00.341 --> 00:00:03.113
Last video ended here, with the power used

00:00:03.173 --> 00:00:05.342
by a home's air conditioner across a hot

00:00:05.362 --> 00:00:05.543
day.

00:00:06.165 --> 00:00:08.609
Power is how fast energy is used, measured

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.995
in kilowatts, and here it peaks at four

00:00:11.036 --> 00:00:12.598
kilowatts at two in the afternoon.

00:00:13.484 --> 00:00:15.334
So how much energy does it use, from

00:00:15.455 --> 00:00:16.863
eight in the morning to eight at night?

00:00:17.648 --> 00:00:19.817
A quick guess is four kilowatts for twelve

00:00:19.858 --> 00:00:21.847
hours, which is forty-eight kilowatt hours.

00:00:22.531 --> 00:00:24.378
Is the true answer more, or less?

00:00:25.035 --> 00:00:25.316
Less,

00:00:25.919 --> 00:00:28.670
because the air conditioner only draws four kilowatts

00:00:28.792 --> 00:00:29.494
at two o'clock.

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:31.725
Here's how to get it exactly.

00:00:32.511 --> 00:00:34.802
Take a thin strip of time, a tiny

00:00:34.843 --> 00:00:35.465
part of an hour.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.374
Over that strip, the power barely changes,

00:00:38.897 --> 00:00:41.141
so the energy is power times time,

00:00:41.644 --> 00:00:44.731
in kilowatts times hours, which is kilowatt hours.

00:00:45.494 --> 00:00:47.321
On the graph, that's the strip's area.

00:00:48.185 --> 00:00:50.650
Add every strip, make them thinner, and you

00:00:50.671 --> 00:00:52.375
get the area under the power graph.

00:00:53.118 --> 00:00:55.570
That's the definite integral of power over the

00:00:55.592 --> 00:00:55.792
day.

00:00:56.819 --> 00:00:58.374
The same idea works for any rate.

00:00:59.001 --> 00:01:01.072
Integrate a rate over an interval, and you

00:01:01.113 --> 00:01:02.500
get the amount it adds up to.

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:05.728
The area's units are the graph's units up,

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:07.352
times its units across.

00:01:08.075 --> 00:01:10.078
So the energy is the integral from zero

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:12.603
to twelve, of four sine of pi t

00:01:12.745 --> 00:01:13.245
over twelve.

00:01:14.148 --> 00:01:16.558
The inside is linear, with rate pi over

00:01:16.579 --> 00:01:18.607
twelve, so the primitive divides by it.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.303
That gives minus forty-eight over pi, times cos

00:01:22.404 --> 00:01:23.729
of pi t over twelve.

00:01:24.453 --> 00:01:26.861
At twelve, cos pi is minus one.

00:01:27.485 --> 00:01:29.239
At zero, cos zero is one.

00:01:29.905 --> 00:01:32.651
So the energy is ninety-six over pi, about

00:01:32.752 --> 00:01:34.355
thirty point six kilowatt hours.

00:01:34.997 --> 00:01:36.987
That's less than forty-eight, as predicted.

00:01:37.715 --> 00:01:38.788
Notice the domain, too.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.640
After eight at night this formula goes negative,

00:01:42.023 --> 00:01:44.150
but an air conditioner can't use negative power.

00:01:44.614 --> 00:01:46.708
So the model only holds from zero to

00:01:46.749 --> 00:01:47.070
twelve.

00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:49.819
Units also stop a common mistake.

00:01:50.502 --> 00:01:52.965
A factory finds that each extra item costs

00:01:53.186 --> 00:01:55.810
fifty minus zero point two x dollars to

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:58.374
make, when it's already making x items.

00:01:59.155 --> 00:02:01.122
That's a rate, in dollars per item.

00:02:01.966 --> 00:02:04.310
Raising production from one hundred to two hundred

00:02:04.451 --> 00:02:07.436
items costs the integral of that rate, from

00:02:07.577 --> 00:02:08.798
one hundred to two hundred.

00:02:09.622 --> 00:02:12.366
The primitive is fifty x minus zero point

00:02:12.508 --> 00:02:14.931
one x squared, and the answer is two

00:02:14.972 --> 00:02:15.593
thousand dollars.

00:02:16.478 --> 00:02:19.411
Dollars per item, times items, gives dollars.

00:02:20.296 --> 00:02:23.145
But integrating a total, like a profit in

00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:25.615
dollars, gives dollars times items.

00:02:26.539 --> 00:02:28.190
That isn't the change in profit you were

00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:28.593
asked for.

00:02:29.259 --> 00:02:31.414
So integrate a rate, and you get an

00:02:31.475 --> 00:02:31.817
amount.

00:02:32.509 --> 00:02:33.593
Some rates go negative.

00:02:34.478 --> 00:02:36.745
Water moves through a gate into a dam

00:02:36.847 --> 00:02:38.792
at a rate of eight minus two t

00:02:38.954 --> 00:02:40.679
megalitres per day, for six days.

00:02:41.423 --> 00:02:43.732
After day four the rate is negative, so

00:02:43.794 --> 00:02:45.340
water flows back out through the gate.

00:02:46.044 --> 00:02:48.207
The integral from zero to six is twelve,

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:50.371
and that's the net change: the dam ends

00:02:50.452 --> 00:02:52.596
up with twelve megalitres more than it started

00:02:52.637 --> 00:02:52.776
with.

00:02:53.378 --> 00:02:54.962
But the water that passed through the gate

00:02:55.144 --> 00:02:58.413
is the total area, sixteen in plus four

00:02:58.575 --> 00:03:00.259
out, which is twenty megalitres.

00:03:00.942 --> 00:03:01.910
So the question decides.

00:03:02.414 --> 00:03:04.520
If it asks how much the amount changed,

00:03:04.902 --> 00:03:05.523
use the integral.

00:03:05.927 --> 00:03:07.877
If it asks how much flowed in total,

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:08.944
use the area.

00:03:09.621 --> 00:03:11.990
Now a region bounded by the y-axis.

00:03:12.773 --> 00:03:14.720
A drainage channel has sides shaped like y

00:03:15.181 --> 00:03:17.911
equals x squared, in metres, with y measured

00:03:17.991 --> 00:03:18.634
up from the bottom.

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:20.913
The water is one metre deep.

00:03:21.756 --> 00:03:24.201
What area of the cross-section, the shape you'd

00:03:24.241 --> 00:03:26.025
see if you cut straight across, does the

00:03:26.065 --> 00:03:26.646
water fill?

00:03:27.529 --> 00:03:29.334
Put the y-axis down the middle of the

00:03:29.355 --> 00:03:29.635
channel.

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:31.842
Then the right half of the water is

00:03:31.903 --> 00:03:34.368
bounded by the curve, the y-axis and the

00:03:34.409 --> 00:03:35.009
water surface.

00:03:35.973 --> 00:03:38.906
Cut it into thin horizontal strips, like layers

00:03:38.928 --> 00:03:39.308
of water.

00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:42.378
At height y, a layer reaches from the

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:45.204
y-axis out to x equals root y.

00:03:46.047 --> 00:03:48.515
So integrate root y with respect to y,

00:03:48.837 --> 00:03:49.839
from zero to one,

00:03:50.362 --> 00:03:51.525
which gives two thirds.

00:03:52.268 --> 00:03:54.733
The channel is even about the y-axis, so

00:03:54.794 --> 00:03:55.114
double it:

00:03:55.636 --> 00:03:56.978
four thirds of a square metre.

00:03:57.628 --> 00:03:59.751
The channel is thirty metres long, so it

00:03:59.812 --> 00:04:03.177
holds four thirds times thirty, which is forty

00:04:03.299 --> 00:04:04.520
cubic metres of water.

00:04:05.385 --> 00:04:07.439
A check with x gives the same answer.

00:04:08.004 --> 00:04:10.585
The two by one rectangle, minus the area

00:04:10.666 --> 00:04:13.567
under the curve, is two minus two thirds,

00:04:13.889 --> 00:04:15.029
which is four thirds.

00:04:15.952 --> 00:04:17.298
And here's a surprise.

00:04:17.902 --> 00:04:20.283
Double the depth to two metres, and the

00:04:20.324 --> 00:04:23.425
water's area nearly triples, because the channel is

00:04:23.566 --> 00:04:24.526
wider higher up.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.602
So the steps for a model are: name

00:04:27.642 --> 00:04:28.703
the rate and its units,

00:04:29.264 --> 00:04:30.865
take the interval from the question,

00:04:31.586 --> 00:04:33.968
integrate, and answer in the units of the

00:04:34.008 --> 00:04:34.309
amount.

00:04:35.054 --> 00:04:35.805
Here's the check to keep.

00:04:36.472 --> 00:04:38.678
Units up, times units across,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.925
tells you what an integral has found.

00:04:41.527 --> 00:04:43.272
If those units don't make the amount you

00:04:43.314 --> 00:04:45.179
want, you're integrating the wrong thing.

00:04:47.827 --> 00:04:48.276
Your turn.

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:51.745
A supplier's hopper pours sand into a box

00:04:51.785 --> 00:04:54.308
trailer at a rate of six t minus

00:04:54.468 --> 00:04:57.311
t squared kilograms per second, for the first

00:04:57.511 --> 00:04:58.252
six seconds.

00:04:58.873 --> 00:05:00.883
How much sand goes into the trailer in

00:05:00.923 --> 00:05:01.405
that time?

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:03.057
Pause here to answer.

00:05:13.829 --> 00:05:16.261
The amount is the integral of the rate,

00:05:16.503 --> 00:05:17.527
from zero to six.

00:05:18.071 --> 00:05:20.785
The primitive is three t squared minus t

00:05:20.827 --> 00:05:21.771
cubed over three.

00:05:22.496 --> 00:05:24.520
At six, that's one hundred and eight minus

00:05:24.562 --> 00:05:26.867
seventy-two, so thirty-six kilograms.

00:05:27.774 --> 00:05:29.369
Fifty-four kilograms is wrong.

00:05:29.773 --> 00:05:32.000
It uses the top rate, nine kilograms per

00:05:32.061 --> 00:05:33.665
second, for the whole six seconds.

00:05:35.298 --> 00:05:35.723
Last one.

00:05:36.575 --> 00:05:38.777
A metal plate is the region bounded by

00:05:39.078 --> 00:05:42.001
y equals x cubed, the y-axis and the

00:05:42.062 --> 00:05:44.684
line y equals eight, measured in centimetres.

00:05:45.547 --> 00:05:48.378
Find its area by integrating with respect to

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:48.819
y.

00:05:49.504 --> 00:05:50.290
Pause here to answer.

00:06:00.938 --> 00:06:01.869
Rearrange the curve.

00:06:02.475 --> 00:06:04.440
x is the cube root of y, so

00:06:04.782 --> 00:06:06.445
x is y to the power of one

00:06:06.546 --> 00:06:06.826
third.

00:06:07.609 --> 00:06:09.894
Integrate from zero to eight, and you get

00:06:10.015 --> 00:06:12.560
three quarters, times y to the power four

00:06:12.641 --> 00:06:12.981
thirds.

00:06:13.945 --> 00:06:16.215
Eight to the four thirds is sixteen, so

00:06:16.256 --> 00:06:18.385
the area is twelve square centimetres.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:20.857
Four square centimetres is wrong.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:22.964
That's the region on the other side of

00:06:22.985 --> 00:06:25.492
the curve, between it and the x-axis.

00:06:27.221 --> 00:06:29.084
So an integral turns a rate into an

00:06:29.125 --> 00:06:31.910
amount, and a region against either axis into

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:32.471
an area,

00:06:33.073 --> 00:06:34.536
as long as the units and the domain

00:06:34.576 --> 00:06:35.077
make sense.

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:37.650
Next, the most important rate of all.

00:06:38.556 --> 00:06:40.368
Here's a robot vacuum at one end of

00:06:40.388 --> 00:06:40.811
a hallway.

00:06:41.534 --> 00:06:43.477
Where is it, how fast is it going,

00:06:43.957 --> 00:06:45.940
and is it speeding up or slowing down?

00:06:48.601 --> 00:06:49.404
Here are the key notes.

00:06:50.047 --> 00:06:51.775
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:51.836 --> 00:06:52.318
to copy them.
